00:02:41.102 --> 00:02:42.310
ALIM KARIM: Thanks, everyone.

00:02:42.310 --> 00:02:44.620
And thanks for, again,
attending an afternoon session.

00:02:44.620 --> 00:02:45.730
So let's get right to it.

00:02:45.730 --> 00:02:47.479
Again, we want to cover
maybe two or three

00:02:47.479 --> 00:02:49.930
slides in the beginning
and really hand it over

00:02:49.930 --> 00:02:53.380
to Jonsi, Devin, and myself,
who'll walk through the demo.

00:02:53.380 --> 00:02:55.960
So before we get too deep
into the technical stuff, what

00:02:55.960 --> 00:02:58.060
we wanted to do was sort
of step back and look

00:02:58.060 --> 00:03:00.550
at this from more a
strategic point of view

00:03:00.550 --> 00:03:03.720
and really understand
the customer context.

00:03:03.720 --> 00:03:06.490
So before we developed Cloud
Volumes, we spent a lot of time

00:03:06.490 --> 00:03:08.770
talking to customers
and understanding

00:03:08.770 --> 00:03:12.310
the nature of problems as it
related to unstructured data,

00:03:12.310 --> 00:03:14.320
files, and public cloud.

00:03:14.320 --> 00:03:17.290
And after analyzing all of
those conversations, what

00:03:17.290 --> 00:03:20.740
we found was you could basically
categorize these problems

00:03:20.740 --> 00:03:23.920
into three main sets, and
each of these three main sets

00:03:23.920 --> 00:03:25.870
had a key persona.

00:03:25.870 --> 00:03:28.210
So first, you had a set of
application administrators,

00:03:28.210 --> 00:03:32.110
application owners that were
trying to move POSIX compliant

00:03:32.110 --> 00:03:35.080
applications from
on premise into GCP,

00:03:35.080 --> 00:03:37.240
and they were doing these
for a variety of reasons.

00:03:37.240 --> 00:03:39.310
Perhaps they were shutting
down a data center.

00:03:39.310 --> 00:03:41.471
Perhaps they had a
cloud first strategy.

00:03:41.471 --> 00:03:43.720
Perhaps they were trying to
modernize the application,

00:03:43.720 --> 00:03:46.870
and the first stage was to
move it to GCP to do that.

00:03:46.870 --> 00:03:48.320
Or perhaps they
were supporting--

00:03:48.320 --> 00:03:50.770
the application was supporting
a business process that

00:03:50.770 --> 00:03:52.840
was variable in
nature, and they wanted

00:03:52.840 --> 00:03:55.420
to take advantage of the
elasticity of the cloud

00:03:55.420 --> 00:03:57.450
to support that
business process.

00:03:57.450 --> 00:03:59.290
So there is a varying,
different things

00:03:59.290 --> 00:04:00.940
on why they were
trying to do this,

00:04:00.940 --> 00:04:04.240
but the lack of enterprise
grade file services in GCP

00:04:04.240 --> 00:04:06.440
essentially left them
with two options.

00:04:06.440 --> 00:04:08.969
One, you go through this
massive architecture process

00:04:08.969 --> 00:04:10.510
even though there
may not be anything

00:04:10.510 --> 00:04:12.490
wrong with the
architecture of your app.

00:04:12.490 --> 00:04:13.180
It's risky.

00:04:13.180 --> 00:04:14.500
It's complex.

00:04:14.500 --> 00:04:17.310
Or you just say simply I'm not
going to move this application.

00:04:17.310 --> 00:04:20.649
So we saw a problem there
that could be solved.

00:04:20.649 --> 00:04:22.510
The second set of
people and problems

00:04:22.510 --> 00:04:24.910
were a set of developers
and DevOps engineers

00:04:24.910 --> 00:04:28.030
that were either trying to
build applications that required

00:04:28.030 --> 00:04:31.150
shared file semantics
in GCP, or they

00:04:31.150 --> 00:04:33.280
were trying to use
workflows that require

00:04:33.280 --> 00:04:35.650
persistent storage in GCP.

00:04:35.650 --> 00:04:38.440
An example we've seen over the
last year, it come up a lot,

00:04:38.440 --> 00:04:40.930
is applications that
are being refactored

00:04:40.930 --> 00:04:45.010
to use containerized platforms
and run on GKE requiring

00:04:45.010 --> 00:04:46.350
persistent storage.

00:04:46.350 --> 00:04:49.160
Cloud volumes is a
good fit for that.

00:04:49.160 --> 00:04:51.610
And then, finally, we have a
set of data analysts and data

00:04:51.610 --> 00:04:53.770
engineers that have
unstructured data

00:04:53.770 --> 00:04:56.410
set on premise that are
trying to take those data

00:04:56.410 --> 00:04:59.728
sets to the cloud to actually
extract value out of those data

00:04:59.728 --> 00:05:01.000
sets.

00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:04.420
What we found common across
all these three problem sets

00:05:04.420 --> 00:05:06.720
is the set of
service expectations.

00:05:06.720 --> 00:05:08.710
So anything that solved
these problems had

00:05:08.710 --> 00:05:12.100
to have these three key things,
and the one that bubbled up

00:05:12.100 --> 00:05:14.350
to the top, this
notion of experience,

00:05:14.350 --> 00:05:16.240
was pretty much the
paramount thing.

00:05:16.240 --> 00:05:18.790
And experience here
has multiple facets.

00:05:18.790 --> 00:05:21.850
It's not just about how well
integrated the product is

00:05:21.850 --> 00:05:24.820
from a look and feel
perspective into GCP,

00:05:24.820 --> 00:05:27.850
but users really wanted
a consistent experience

00:05:27.850 --> 00:05:28.570
with Google.

00:05:28.570 --> 00:05:30.250
So that means support.

00:05:30.250 --> 00:05:31.510
That means billing.

00:05:31.510 --> 00:05:34.870
That means operations and a
multitude of other product

00:05:34.870 --> 00:05:38.050
related aspects deeply
integrated into GCP,

00:05:38.050 --> 00:05:41.500
and that's what we focused on
to bring with Cloud Volumes.

00:05:41.500 --> 00:05:44.140
The other two pieces around
consumption and economics

00:05:44.140 --> 00:05:45.880
actually go hand-in-hand.

00:05:45.880 --> 00:05:48.640
So with the ability
for this thing

00:05:48.640 --> 00:05:51.250
being a true cloud service,
they expect consumption

00:05:51.250 --> 00:05:53.927
to be able to go up and
down, and the economics

00:05:53.927 --> 00:05:55.010
should match that pattern.

00:05:58.227 --> 00:05:59.810
So now, let's talk
about Cloud Volumes

00:05:59.810 --> 00:06:02.030
for GCP and the various
elements involved.

00:06:02.030 --> 00:06:04.692
And again, this is more of
a service overview slide,

00:06:04.692 --> 00:06:06.650
and we'll talk about the
technical details here

00:06:06.650 --> 00:06:07.920
in a second.

00:06:07.920 --> 00:06:10.670
But the simplest way to think
about cloud volumes for GCP

00:06:10.670 --> 00:06:14.060
is that they are a simple
to consume performant

00:06:14.060 --> 00:06:16.370
NFS or SMB endpoint.

00:06:16.370 --> 00:06:19.790
It's a no op service, so
you as an application owner

00:06:19.790 --> 00:06:22.310
or as a developer
simply just consume

00:06:22.310 --> 00:06:24.326
that NFS or SMB endpoint.

00:06:24.326 --> 00:06:25.700
You don't have to
worry about any

00:06:25.700 --> 00:06:27.440
of the infrastructure
related tasks

00:06:27.440 --> 00:06:30.440
around setting things up,
patching things, maintaining

00:06:30.440 --> 00:06:32.010
things, scaling things.

00:06:32.010 --> 00:06:34.760
That's handled seamlessly
between NetApp and Google.

00:06:34.760 --> 00:06:37.220
You just get the
NFS or SMB endpoint

00:06:37.220 --> 00:06:39.620
that you're interested in.

00:06:39.620 --> 00:06:42.650
The second thing around native
user experience-- and the demo

00:06:42.650 --> 00:06:44.790
will really show you
what we mean by that.

00:06:44.790 --> 00:06:47.690
But the engineering teams
have spent a ton of time

00:06:47.690 --> 00:06:50.750
integrating this so that
it looks and feels exactly

00:06:50.750 --> 00:06:53.780
like any other Google
service to the point where

00:06:53.780 --> 00:06:55.310
there's no separate punchouts.

00:06:55.310 --> 00:06:56.990
There's no separate UIs.

00:06:56.990 --> 00:06:59.480
How you authenticate into
the Google Cloud console

00:06:59.480 --> 00:07:02.010
stays consistent as you
move into the service,

00:07:02.010 --> 00:07:04.920
so there's a security element
to this integration as well.

00:07:04.920 --> 00:07:06.830
And I can describe
it all I want,

00:07:06.830 --> 00:07:09.710
but you'll get to
see it firsthand.

00:07:09.710 --> 00:07:12.230
So NetApp has had a rich
history in data management

00:07:12.230 --> 00:07:13.730
over the last 20
years, and we're

00:07:13.730 --> 00:07:16.190
bringing all of that
goodness to Cloud Volumes--

00:07:16.190 --> 00:07:18.500
the ability to take
snapshots, the ability

00:07:18.500 --> 00:07:21.140
to then create rapid copies
out of these snapshots,

00:07:21.140 --> 00:07:22.670
and the ability to
synchronize data.

00:07:22.670 --> 00:07:25.460
That's all coming
to Cloud Volumes.

00:07:25.460 --> 00:07:29.060
Support is another thing we
hear from customers about a lot.

00:07:29.060 --> 00:07:31.010
Oftentimes, when we
talk about new products,

00:07:31.010 --> 00:07:32.620
we focus on the beginning.

00:07:32.620 --> 00:07:34.760
It's like, what does the
user interface look like,

00:07:34.760 --> 00:07:36.120
and how well is that integrated?

00:07:36.120 --> 00:07:39.530
But we have to think about the
lifecycle of the product right.

00:07:39.530 --> 00:07:41.690
And things will go
wrong, and customers

00:07:41.690 --> 00:07:44.000
want to know that there
is an integrated support

00:07:44.000 --> 00:07:47.270
system to help them
when things do go wrong.

00:07:47.270 --> 00:07:49.280
So with Cloud Volumes
for GCP, support

00:07:49.280 --> 00:07:52.460
is actually integrated with
Google in that L-1 support is

00:07:52.460 --> 00:07:53.840
offered by Google.

00:07:53.840 --> 00:07:55.820
Google always answers
the support call,

00:07:55.820 --> 00:07:58.790
and Google always maintains
the customer relationship

00:07:58.790 --> 00:08:00.170
with the customer.

00:08:00.170 --> 00:08:02.000
They may escalate to
NetApp in the back,

00:08:02.000 --> 00:08:05.725
but Google always owns
the customer relationship.

00:08:05.725 --> 00:08:07.850
Moving on to some of the
elements on the right-hand

00:08:07.850 --> 00:08:08.550
side--

00:08:08.550 --> 00:08:10.340
so this is a cloud service.

00:08:10.340 --> 00:08:13.070
It offers a true pay-go
consumption model.

00:08:13.070 --> 00:08:15.260
It's based on
allocated capacity,

00:08:15.260 --> 00:08:18.290
and you can vary the amount
of allocated capacity

00:08:18.290 --> 00:08:20.760
over the course of a month.

00:08:20.760 --> 00:08:23.290
Billing is integrated
into GCP as well,

00:08:23.290 --> 00:08:25.260
so all of that
allocated capacity

00:08:25.260 --> 00:08:28.920
that you consume over a month is
billed directly through Google.

00:08:28.920 --> 00:08:32.100
You don't get any other separate
invoices or things from NetApp.

00:08:32.100 --> 00:08:34.559
Everything happens
through GCP, again,

00:08:34.559 --> 00:08:37.419
keeping within that
same ecosystem.

00:08:37.419 --> 00:08:39.900
And then, finally, we're going
to enable hybrid enablement

00:08:39.900 --> 00:08:42.480
for the service in that we're
going to allow customers

00:08:42.480 --> 00:08:45.720
to move data both from on
premise and other SMB or NFS

00:08:45.720 --> 00:08:47.915
services into Cloud Volumes.

00:08:47.915 --> 00:08:49.290
And then the region
piece-- we're

00:08:49.290 --> 00:08:51.990
going to start this
service in US East 4.

00:08:51.990 --> 00:08:54.164
We're going to roll out
to other regions in the US

00:08:54.164 --> 00:08:56.580
by the end of the year, as
well as some regions in Europe,

00:08:56.580 --> 00:08:59.040
and we'll cover that towards
the end of the presentation.

00:09:02.190 --> 00:09:06.240
So one thing that is very unique
about Cloud Volumes for GCP

00:09:06.240 --> 00:09:08.040
is this notion of
service levels,

00:09:08.040 --> 00:09:11.320
and so it bears a few
minutes worth talking about.

00:09:11.320 --> 00:09:14.310
So think about Cloud Volumes
coming in three flavors,

00:09:14.310 --> 00:09:17.040
and these three flavors
are identical in terms

00:09:17.040 --> 00:09:19.680
of the protocols they offer,
as well as the rich data

00:09:19.680 --> 00:09:21.190
management features
that they offer.

00:09:21.190 --> 00:09:24.510
So across these three
flavors, you get SMB or NFS.

00:09:24.510 --> 00:09:25.590
You get snapshots.

00:09:25.590 --> 00:09:26.460
You get copies.

00:09:26.460 --> 00:09:28.380
You get data synchronization.

00:09:28.380 --> 00:09:30.890
How these three
service levels differ

00:09:30.890 --> 00:09:32.970
is in the amount of
performance that you

00:09:32.970 --> 00:09:36.450
get per allocated
terabyte of capacity.

00:09:36.450 --> 00:09:39.040
So the three service
levels are listed here--

00:09:39.040 --> 00:09:40.680
standard, premium, and extreme.

00:09:40.680 --> 00:09:42.900
In the case of standard,
you get 16 megabytes

00:09:42.900 --> 00:09:44.610
per terabyte allocated.

00:09:44.610 --> 00:09:45.930
In the case of premium--

00:09:45.930 --> 00:09:48.270
64 megabytes per
terabyte allocated.

00:09:48.270 --> 00:09:49.500
And in the case of extreme--

00:09:49.500 --> 00:09:52.740
128 megabytes per
terabyte allocated.

00:09:52.740 --> 00:09:53.886
Why did we do this?

00:09:53.886 --> 00:09:55.260
We did this because
customers are

00:09:55.260 --> 00:09:58.270
going to use Cloud Volumes
for different use cases.

00:09:58.270 --> 00:10:00.240
So if you're doing
virtualized applications,

00:10:00.240 --> 00:10:02.310
if you're doing general
purpose databases,

00:10:02.310 --> 00:10:04.389
premium is probably
where you want to start.

00:10:04.389 --> 00:10:06.180
If you're doing high
performance computing,

00:10:06.180 --> 00:10:08.430
if you're doing
genomic analysis,

00:10:08.430 --> 00:10:10.260
extreme is where you want to go.

00:10:10.260 --> 00:10:11.919
But we also give
you the flexibility

00:10:11.919 --> 00:10:13.710
to change these tiers
because oftentimes we

00:10:13.710 --> 00:10:17.040
hear from customers-- we kind
of know, but we're not sure.

00:10:17.040 --> 00:10:18.480
So start off with
one, and you're

00:10:18.480 --> 00:10:19.854
free to change it
to another one.

00:10:23.020 --> 00:10:25.770
So before we go into the demo,
just one quick technical slide

00:10:25.770 --> 00:10:27.960
on what the building
blocks are and what

00:10:27.960 --> 00:10:30.160
this architecture looks like.

00:10:30.160 --> 00:10:32.730
So in this case,
on the far side,

00:10:32.730 --> 00:10:34.130
we have the Cloud
Volume service.

00:10:34.130 --> 00:10:36.960
This is where you provision
and get your volumes from.

00:10:36.960 --> 00:10:38.790
On this side, on
the left-hand side,

00:10:38.790 --> 00:10:41.190
we actually have a GCP project.

00:10:41.190 --> 00:10:42.690
You have a region
in that project.

00:10:42.690 --> 00:10:45.510
You have a set of instances
within a given VPC,

00:10:45.510 --> 00:10:48.540
and all of those instances
have access to cloud volumes.

00:10:48.540 --> 00:10:51.450
And you can control this
access using export policies,

00:10:51.450 --> 00:10:53.760
and you'll see this in
the demo in terms of how

00:10:53.760 --> 00:10:55.507
we secure these cloud volumes.

00:10:55.507 --> 00:10:57.090
The key thing I want
to point out here

00:10:57.090 --> 00:11:00.810
is that cloud volumes are a
regionally accessible resource.

00:11:00.810 --> 00:11:03.420
So even if you have
compute incidences

00:11:03.420 --> 00:11:06.450
in zone one, zone two, zone
three in a given region,

00:11:06.450 --> 00:11:09.450
all of those compute instances
can access cloud volumes

00:11:09.450 --> 00:11:12.030
in that given region.

00:11:12.030 --> 00:11:13.980
So with that, the
engineering guys

00:11:13.980 --> 00:11:16.710
told me don't go more
than three or four slides,

00:11:16.710 --> 00:11:17.844
