00:21:07.860 --> 00:21:10.380
it a little bit in terms
of the discovery piece,

00:21:10.380 --> 00:21:12.921
so you need to be a project
owner to enable the service.

00:21:12.921 --> 00:21:14.670
Because now you're
talking about something

00:21:14.670 --> 00:21:16.830
that's a billable thing.

00:21:16.830 --> 00:21:19.140
So you need to be an
owner to enable it,

00:21:19.140 --> 00:21:20.840
but once you have
done that, then it's

00:21:20.840 --> 00:21:23.340
like editors in the
project have access

00:21:23.340 --> 00:21:24.780
to be able to go create it.

00:21:24.780 --> 00:21:27.390
We do have some thoughts
around how we introduced

00:21:27.390 --> 00:21:32.760
more granular IAM, like
down to create, update,

00:21:32.760 --> 00:21:34.740
delete-type permissions,
but that's something

00:21:34.740 --> 00:21:37.704
that we introduce later on.

00:21:37.704 --> 00:21:41.200
AUDIENCE: So is this a physical
filer sitting in a data center

00:21:41.200 --> 00:21:44.540
somewhere, or is it virtual?

00:21:44.540 --> 00:21:47.157
ALIM KARIM: Yeah, so right
now it's a physical filer.

00:21:47.157 --> 00:21:48.740
AUDIENCE: Can you
repeat the question?

00:21:48.740 --> 00:21:50.365
DEVIN LEHMAN: Is this
a physical filer,

00:21:50.365 --> 00:21:52.450
or is it sitting somewhere
in a data center--

00:21:52.450 --> 00:21:55.870
or, sorry, in the pod.

00:21:55.870 --> 00:22:00.790
JONSI STEFANSSON: So I mean,
the software aspect is--

00:22:00.790 --> 00:22:04.030
we have multiple service
levels, and service level

00:22:04.030 --> 00:22:06.070
might, for example,
be-- the lower service

00:22:06.070 --> 00:22:07.570
level might be pure software.

00:22:07.570 --> 00:22:11.110
And then premium-- if you want
to rate select, for example,

00:22:11.110 --> 00:22:14.580
with genome sequencing,
we had an amazing company

00:22:14.580 --> 00:22:16.720
as one of our private
preview customers

00:22:16.720 --> 00:22:22.230
that really needed
high-performance storage

00:22:22.230 --> 00:22:24.490
to do genome sequencing.

00:22:24.490 --> 00:22:30.520
So we would never have been able
to meet that on pure software.

00:22:30.520 --> 00:22:36.369
So I think the top tiers are
always going to be hardware.

00:22:36.369 --> 00:22:38.285
ALIM KARIM: I think the
way we want it extract

00:22:38.285 --> 00:22:43.060
is away from customers, is give
them an SLO-based endpoint,

00:22:43.060 --> 00:22:45.220
and then it's up
to us with Google

00:22:45.220 --> 00:22:49.086
to engineer the data
plane to meet that SLO.

00:22:49.086 --> 00:22:52.530
AUDIENCE: Yeah, I was just
thinking, OK, there's a globe.

00:22:52.530 --> 00:22:58.142
How many physical filers
are in proximity with SMP,

00:22:58.142 --> 00:23:01.410
and when do I potentially
run out of demand?

00:23:01.410 --> 00:23:03.680
ALIM KARIM: That's
up to us to manage.

00:23:03.680 --> 00:23:05.480
And hence it being
a cloud service--

00:23:05.480 --> 00:23:06.813
AUDIENCE: None of your business.

00:23:06.813 --> 00:23:08.460
[LAUGHTER]

00:23:08.460 --> 00:23:09.920
ALIM KARIM: Yeah,
we want customers

00:23:09.920 --> 00:23:14.120
to focus on the endpoint and
the SLO of that endpoint,

00:23:14.120 --> 00:23:16.670
and leave it to us to
worry about-- or have

00:23:16.670 --> 00:23:18.202
sleepless nights.

00:23:18.202 --> 00:23:20.130
AUDIENCE: If you've
got 7 petabytes,

00:23:20.130 --> 00:23:22.759
maybe you should call.

00:23:22.759 --> 00:23:24.800
ALIM KARIM: We'll have to
put something in the UI

00:23:24.800 --> 00:23:30.862
that says, you know, too
many zeros, dial this number.

00:23:30.862 --> 00:23:34.686
AUDIENCE: Two questions,
first one on the two tiers

00:23:34.686 --> 00:23:37.560
that you have.

00:23:37.560 --> 00:23:38.812
ALIM KARIM: Three, right?

00:23:38.812 --> 00:23:41.192
AUDIENCE: Yeah,
I saw the space--

00:23:41.192 --> 00:23:43.096
a factor of space there.

00:23:43.096 --> 00:23:45.480
So are you saying
it's a scale measure--

00:23:45.480 --> 00:23:46.730
JONSI STEFANSSON: Yeah.

00:23:46.730 --> 00:23:52.990
Yeah, so for example, if you
provision a 10 terabyte volume,

00:23:52.990 --> 00:24:00.077
you're actually getting 10 times
128, so 1.2 gigabytes a second.

00:24:00.077 --> 00:24:01.660
So we've actually
been able to live up

00:24:01.660 --> 00:24:03.550
to that, which is
awesome, and we actually

00:24:03.550 --> 00:24:06.610
have a customer here that
actually reached higher

00:24:06.610 --> 00:24:07.720
than that.

00:24:07.720 --> 00:24:13.790
But we sort of flatten out
around 12 to 14 terabytes.

00:24:13.790 --> 00:24:16.460
That's when-- it doesn't
scale endlessly, of course,

00:24:16.460 --> 00:24:19.930
but that's where we're
sort of capping out.

00:24:19.930 --> 00:24:23.631
It's linear until that point,
and then it fattens out.

00:24:23.631 --> 00:24:25.595
AUDIENCE: Second
question was around,

00:24:25.595 --> 00:24:30.908
when you simply announced Google
Cloud Firestore, [INAUDIBLE]

00:24:30.908 --> 00:24:33.343
have here, so how
do you sort of see

00:24:33.343 --> 00:24:36.642
those two things shaping up?

00:24:36.642 --> 00:24:38.600
JONSI STEFANSSON: Devin,
you want to take that?

00:24:38.600 --> 00:24:39.730
You're from Google.

00:24:39.730 --> 00:24:40.320
DEVIN LEHMAN: Yeah, absolutely.

00:24:40.320 --> 00:24:41.750
ALIM KARIM: Let the
Google man answer this.

00:24:41.750 --> 00:24:43.700
DEVIN LEHMAN: OK, so
yeah, we can all kind of

00:24:43.700 --> 00:24:47.860
take a stab at this
one, but the usage

00:24:47.860 --> 00:24:50.489
coming from those
particular users,

00:24:50.489 --> 00:24:52.030
so people who are
using NetApp aren't

00:24:52.030 --> 00:24:53.980
all of a sudden going to jump
ship and use a completely

00:24:53.980 --> 00:24:55.330
different filer technology.

00:24:55.330 --> 00:24:56.746
LookSee's a great
example of that.

00:24:56.746 --> 00:24:59.699
They've been very,
very awesome in being

00:24:59.699 --> 00:25:01.990
co-announcers of our particular
partnership in general.

00:25:01.990 --> 00:25:04.240
They're using ONTAP solution,
and they're very excited

00:25:04.240 --> 00:25:06.130
a GCP solution as well.

00:25:06.130 --> 00:25:08.164
For particular new
users, maybe SMB markets,

00:25:08.164 --> 00:25:10.330
or those who are going
through a startup phase, that

00:25:10.330 --> 00:25:12.746
aren't going to drop a bunch
of gear and a particular data

00:25:12.746 --> 00:25:14.560
center might use
Filestore, but there's

00:25:14.560 --> 00:25:15.790
a yin and yang from
which solutions

00:25:15.790 --> 00:25:16.990
you're probably going
to end up using.

00:25:16.990 --> 00:25:19.130
ALIM KARIM: And that's, from a
customer perspective, you know,

00:25:19.130 --> 00:25:21.640
we've had that question come
up, and customers have kind of

00:25:21.640 --> 00:25:25.570
given us an answer, which is
for basic needs, yeah, NFS, V3,

00:25:25.570 --> 00:25:27.350
whatever filestore is OK.

00:25:27.350 --> 00:25:30.400
But as soon as you start
talking about a service that

00:25:30.400 --> 00:25:34.030
is more feature-oriented, like
this one, where you can change

00:25:34.030 --> 00:25:36.520
service levels, you can have
advanced data management

00:25:36.520 --> 00:25:39.790
features, you can have
true multi-protocol

00:25:39.790 --> 00:25:41.110
from a single volume.

00:25:41.110 --> 00:25:43.390
These are kind of things where--

00:25:43.390 --> 00:25:46.270
earlier when I talked about the
customer context, and customers

00:25:46.270 --> 00:25:48.940
wanting to move applications
that were file-base,

00:25:48.940 --> 00:25:51.100
POSIX-based, to the
cloud, these are the kind

00:25:51.100 --> 00:25:53.470
of services that they need.

00:25:53.470 --> 00:25:56.230
If it was just about
providing a simple NFS share,

00:25:56.230 --> 00:25:59.320
you stand up a single-node
file server and you have that.

00:25:59.320 --> 00:26:00.910
You don't even need a service.

00:26:00.910 --> 00:26:03.430
You need a service when it
gets into the enterprise

00:26:03.430 --> 00:26:04.750
with these kind of features.

00:26:04.750 --> 00:26:05.830
But Mavinder, you can--

00:26:05.830 --> 00:26:07.955
MAVINDER SINGH: Well, you
kind of stole my thunder,

00:26:07.955 --> 00:26:10.060
but from our point
of view at Google,

00:26:10.060 --> 00:26:12.500
I think this is all
about customer choice,

00:26:12.500 --> 00:26:14.950
so having multiple
services with some features

00:26:14.950 --> 00:26:16.820
being available in a
particular service.

00:26:16.820 --> 00:26:19.320
It helps customers, and that's
what we're working towards.

00:26:19.320 --> 00:26:21.712
Today if you're looking
for, for example, SMB,

00:26:21.712 --> 00:26:23.170
you can do that
with Filestore, you

00:26:23.170 --> 00:26:25.150
can do that with Cloud Volumes.

00:26:25.150 --> 00:26:26.860
To give you an
example, if you're

00:26:26.860 --> 00:26:28.480
looking for a non-SQL
database, there

00:26:28.480 --> 00:26:30.710
are multiple non-SQL
database options

00:26:30.710 --> 00:26:32.210
that are available
to our customers,

00:26:32.210 --> 00:26:33.850
and so in the
similar vein, we're

00:26:33.850 --> 00:26:36.804
working on both the
offerings for our customers.

00:26:41.172 --> 00:26:42.380
DEVIN LEHMAN: Good questions.

00:26:42.380 --> 00:26:43.402
JONSI STEFANSSON: Yeah.

00:26:43.402 --> 00:26:43.840
DEVIN LEHMAN: Cool.

00:26:43.840 --> 00:26:44.714
ALIM KARIM: Go ahead.

00:26:44.714 --> 00:26:46.395
AUDIENCE: How does
the pricing forecast?

00:26:46.395 --> 00:26:50.201
Sounds like in the demo you're
able to toggle those three

00:26:50.201 --> 00:26:50.700
options.

00:26:50.700 --> 00:26:52.857
[INAUDIBLE]

00:26:52.857 --> 00:26:54.690
ALIM KARIM: So something
the product manager

00:26:54.690 --> 00:26:55.920
should have covered earlier.

00:26:55.920 --> 00:26:57.170
JONSI STEFANSSON: Where is he?

00:26:57.170 --> 00:26:59.185
Oh, he's here?

00:26:59.185 --> 00:27:01.050
ALIM KARIM: Yeah, so
the pricing is actually

00:27:01.050 --> 00:27:03.090
separate for each of
the service tiers.

00:27:03.090 --> 00:27:06.630
And how we do this is based
on allocated capacity,

00:27:06.630 --> 00:27:09.570
but the-- call it the skew--

00:27:09.570 --> 00:27:10.720
is changeable.

00:27:10.720 --> 00:27:12.750
So if you allocate
5 terabytes today

00:27:12.750 --> 00:27:17.760
of Premium, and in one week
you change that to be Extreme,

00:27:17.760 --> 00:27:20.310
we track the skew with
Google in the back end.

00:27:20.310 --> 00:27:22.710
So the billing engine
will bill you one week's

00:27:22.710 --> 00:27:24.930
worth of usage on
Premium, and then

00:27:24.930 --> 00:27:27.120
one week's worth of
usage on Extreme,

00:27:27.120 --> 00:27:29.680
or whatever your
capacity ends up being.

00:27:29.680 --> 00:27:34.354
So the flexibility is mirrored
by the underlying economics.

00:27:34.354 --> 00:27:38.210
AUDIENCE: I see, so you commit
to that month, but it skews it.

00:27:38.210 --> 00:27:39.821
ALIM KARIM: Yeah, so it's--

00:27:39.821 --> 00:27:40.320
right.

00:27:40.320 --> 00:27:42.570
So it's true pay-go in
the sense that if you

00:27:42.570 --> 00:27:44.670
start with 5
terabytes of Premium,

00:27:44.670 --> 00:27:47.310
you can change that to
5 terabytes of Extreme

00:27:47.310 --> 00:27:49.080
in the middle of the month.

00:27:49.080 --> 00:27:53.530
So if Premium is $0.30 a gig,
and Extreme is $0.40 a gig,

00:27:53.530 --> 00:27:56.250
it will be the average of those
two is what you're going to pay

00:27:56.250 --> 00:27:57.250
at the end of the month.

00:27:59.688 --> 00:28:03.857
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:28:03.857 --> 00:28:05.190
ALIM KARIM: Which storage media?

00:28:05.190 --> 00:28:09.210
Something that gives you
either 16, 64, or 128 megabytes

00:28:09.210 --> 00:28:10.571
per terabyte stored.

00:28:10.571 --> 00:28:12.154
AUDIENCE: Still none
of your business.

00:28:12.154 --> 00:28:13.597
[LAUGHTER]

00:28:14.560 --> 00:28:17.270
ALIM KARIM: Again, focus
on the performance SLO.

00:28:22.870 --> 00:28:25.260
AUDIENCE: Can you shed
light on projects?

00:28:25.260 --> 00:28:26.680
ALIM KARIM: No,
not at the moment.

00:28:26.680 --> 00:28:30.690
And we wanted to keep this
consistent with how GCP does

00:28:30.690 --> 00:28:32.220
resource management.

00:28:32.220 --> 00:28:35.970
And that's been-- to Jonsi's
earlier point, the core design

00:28:35.970 --> 00:28:36.900
principle.

00:28:36.900 --> 00:28:39.450
Keep it like a resource
within a project that's

00:28:39.450 --> 00:28:41.130
isolated within a project.

00:28:41.130 --> 00:28:44.130
And we haven't really come
up with any kind of use cases

00:28:44.130 --> 00:28:48.360
or customer information that
said across-project sharing is

00:28:48.360 --> 00:28:48.930
needed.

00:28:48.930 --> 00:28:49.110
I don't know.

00:28:49.110 --> 00:28:50.401
Jonsi, you want to add to that?

00:28:50.401 --> 00:28:52.054
JONSI STEFANSSON:
Yeah, actually this

00:28:52.054 --> 00:28:53.970
is something that we
really want to look into.

00:28:53.970 --> 00:28:56.971
Because let's say you have
your development team that

00:28:56.971 --> 00:28:59.220
is actually developing, and
then they want to push it,

00:28:59.220 --> 00:29:03.930
and they want to take advantage
of being able to clone a volume

00:29:03.930 --> 00:29:06.510
that's going from development,
to staging, and then

00:29:06.510 --> 00:29:09.750
to production, and that might
be totally different project.

00:29:09.750 --> 00:29:11.650
So I really understand--

00:29:11.650 --> 00:29:14.220
as a developer, I
really understand

00:29:14.220 --> 00:29:17.160
the flow should really
be there, and that's

00:29:17.160 --> 00:29:18.690
what we're really looking into.

00:29:18.690 --> 00:29:21.273
I'm really glad you asked that
question, because I've actually

00:29:21.273 --> 00:29:23.820
been fighting to actually
get it implemented.

00:29:23.820 --> 00:29:26.931
So I really get the
use case for that.

00:29:26.931 --> 00:29:29.055
AUDIENCE: When you're using
the word "project," are

00:29:29.055 --> 00:29:32.730
you talking about XPN and those
projects or service projects?

00:29:32.730 --> 00:29:35.990
Are you limiting it
to a specific network?

00:29:35.990 --> 00:29:36.740
ALIM KARIM: Right.

00:29:36.740 --> 00:29:38.260
Good question.

00:29:38.260 --> 00:29:42.110
So the question was that is
this an XPN shared project?

00:29:42.110 --> 00:29:44.970
In Google, you have the ability
to create a parent project,

00:29:44.970 --> 00:29:46.440
and then child
projects underneath,

00:29:46.440 --> 00:29:48.990
with shared resources,
firewall rules, and such.

00:29:48.990 --> 00:29:51.840
So right now, we're talking
about a flat topology.

00:29:51.840 --> 00:29:54.570
So it's the services
available within the project

00:29:54.570 --> 00:29:57.210
that you provision in that.

00:29:57.210 --> 00:29:58.961
The ability to do
cross-project networking

00:29:58.961 --> 00:30:01.418
and actually have a hierarchy
of projects, that's something

00:30:01.418 --> 00:30:02.910
we're going to introduce later.

00:30:02.910 --> 00:30:05.550
There's really-- there's
some technical things,

00:30:05.550 --> 00:30:09.090
but mostly it's just testing
and QA'ing that setup,

00:30:09.090 --> 00:30:11.170
and making sure it works.

00:30:11.170 --> 00:30:14.130
So at the moment, think about
it as a flat in-project--

00:30:14.130 --> 00:30:17.205
in-the-same-project resource.

00:30:17.205 --> 00:30:20.982
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:30:20.982 --> 00:30:21.690
ALIM KARIM: Yeah.

00:30:21.690 --> 00:30:22.926
JONSI STEFANSSON: Yeah.

00:30:22.926 --> 00:30:24.967
ALIM KARIM: More for
testing things, like I said.

00:30:24.967 --> 00:30:27.949
AUDIENCE: So how will
you handle some use cases

00:30:27.949 --> 00:30:33.629
where they need the performance
but not the capacity?

00:30:33.629 --> 00:30:35.170
ALIM KARIM: They
need the performance

00:30:35.170 --> 00:30:36.920
but they don't
need the capacity?

00:30:36.920 --> 00:30:40.780
So then-- my answer, and
then I'm sure Jonsi has his,

00:30:40.780 --> 00:30:43.510
but my answer is that when
you're procuring a cloud

00:30:43.510 --> 00:30:46.060
volume, you're actually
paying for a resource that

00:30:46.060 --> 00:30:47.440
has two dimensions.

00:30:47.440 --> 00:30:50.350
You have a capacity dimension
and a performance dimension.

00:30:50.350 --> 00:30:54.800
And you're going to pay
for what you need the most.

00:30:54.800 --> 00:30:57.280
So if you need capacity, you're
going to pay for capacity.

00:30:57.280 --> 00:31:00.430
If you need performance, you're
going to over-allocate capacity

00:31:00.430 --> 00:31:02.770
in order to get the
performance you need.

00:31:02.770 --> 00:31:04.720
That's why we also have
service tiers to be

00:31:04.720 --> 00:31:07.210
able to be a little bit
more flexible with that,

00:31:07.210 --> 00:31:10.550
and not so rigid,
but in some cases,

00:31:10.550 --> 00:31:12.860
you're going to have
to over-provision.

00:31:12.860 --> 00:31:15.550
JONSI STEFANSSON: Yeah, I mean,
we were really, really lucky

00:31:15.550 --> 00:31:19.210
when it came to private
preview customers.

00:31:19.210 --> 00:31:23.184
We basically got every
single flavor of workload,

00:31:23.184 --> 00:31:24.850
so we could actually
work really closely

00:31:24.850 --> 00:31:27.320
with all those customers.

00:31:27.320 --> 00:31:32.950
One of them was running
Oracle databases on file,

00:31:32.950 --> 00:31:35.470
and they've always been running
that on block, for example.

00:31:35.470 --> 00:31:36.970
But they were
actually clustering it

00:31:36.970 --> 00:31:39.250
so it was multi-host-attached,
so they are actually

00:31:39.250 --> 00:31:46.030
getting way better performance
or throughput on flash.

00:31:46.030 --> 00:31:48.340
We saw that they were
over-provisioning.

00:31:48.340 --> 00:31:50.800
Even though the database
might have been, like,

00:31:50.800 --> 00:31:53.410
a terabyte, almost
all of them, they

00:31:53.410 --> 00:31:56.854
were provisioning 10, 12,
14 terabytes to actually get

00:31:56.854 --> 00:31:57.520
the performance.

00:31:57.520 --> 00:32:02.260
So to your point, what
was the biggest value

00:32:02.260 --> 00:32:05.080
for them was actually
finally they actually

00:32:05.080 --> 00:32:08.370
could run those Oracle
workloads in the public load.

00:32:08.370 --> 00:32:14.210
And they hadn't been able to do
that until with Cloud Volumes.

00:32:14.210 --> 00:32:17.850
So it was actually--

00:32:17.850 --> 00:32:23.470
you can see that
you're paying for way

00:32:23.470 --> 00:32:25.687
more capacity than you're
actually using, but--

00:32:25.687 --> 00:32:28.020
ALIM KARIM: Sometimes you
have to pay for what you need,

00:32:28.020 --> 00:32:29.020
I guess.

00:32:29.020 --> 00:32:30.910
In this case, you
need performance.

00:32:30.910 --> 00:32:32.140
DEVIN LEHMAN: I'd like just to
highlight that really quickly,

00:32:32.140 --> 00:32:32.320
too.

00:32:32.320 --> 00:32:34.528
So working with NetApp in
this particular partnership

00:32:34.528 --> 00:32:36.550
has been instrumental
in getting projects

00:32:36.550 --> 00:32:37.630
like this off the ground.

00:32:37.630 --> 00:32:40.096
So their customers are
fantastic with feedback.

00:32:40.096 --> 00:32:42.470
Their customers are fantastic
in saying what doesn't work

00:32:42.470 --> 00:32:43.261
and what does work.

00:32:43.261 --> 00:32:45.160
And the antithesis of
that is what you're

00:32:45.160 --> 00:32:47.730
seeing on the screen today.

00:32:47.730 --> 00:32:50.016
ALIM KARIM: There
was another question.

00:32:50.016 --> 00:32:52.426
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:33:00.715 --> 00:33:01.590
JONSI STEFANSSON: No.

00:33:01.590 --> 00:33:05.600
So we already have, like
in our staging environment

00:33:05.600 --> 00:33:07.735
with Google, we already
have Cloud Sync.

00:33:07.735 --> 00:33:08.909
We're with the Cloud Sync.

00:33:08.909 --> 00:33:11.200
That actually can synchronize
from endpoint to endpoint

00:33:11.200 --> 00:33:14.507
and is agnostic when
it comes to filers.

00:33:14.507 --> 00:33:17.090
If you're asking about something
like Snap Mirror or something

00:33:17.090 --> 00:33:20.360
that's actually retaining
[INAUDIBLE] duplication

00:33:20.360 --> 00:33:23.990
and compression
during transfer--

00:33:23.990 --> 00:33:25.340
AUDIENCE: It's not the same.

00:33:25.340 --> 00:33:28.439
JONSI STEFANSSON: No,
it's not, but I mean,

00:33:28.439 --> 00:33:30.480
that's something that we
are really looking into.

00:33:30.480 --> 00:33:36.020
But the thing is it's actually
a single-tenant environment,

00:33:36.020 --> 00:33:39.120
a cluster-wide variable that is
being pushed to a multi-tenant,

00:33:39.120 --> 00:33:41.070
and Google would just
say, are you guys crazy?

00:33:41.070 --> 00:33:44.700
You're not-- this would be
a breach of all security

00:33:44.700 --> 00:33:46.980
protocols, basically, because
you're taking something

00:33:46.980 --> 00:33:49.890
from a single-tenant, and the
way it's actually required.

00:33:49.890 --> 00:33:52.170
But NetApp and Google,
we are actually

00:33:52.170 --> 00:33:54.660
working on reducing
the granularity of that

00:33:54.660 --> 00:33:57.090
so you can actually set
it on a volume level.

00:33:57.090 --> 00:34:00.850
And if that's the case, then
we can introduce Snap Mirror.

00:34:00.850 --> 00:34:02.890
Probably revealing
something that I'm not

00:34:02.890 --> 00:34:04.876
allowed to, like always, but--

00:34:04.876 --> 00:34:07.780
[LAUGHTER]

00:34:07.780 --> 00:34:08.950
ALIM KARIM: Sure, go ahead.

00:34:08.950 --> 00:34:11.200
AUDIENCE: When you tier your
different service levels,

00:34:11.200 --> 00:34:13.104
is it actually
migrating the volumes,

00:34:13.104 --> 00:34:16.065
or is it just [INAUDIBLE]?

00:34:16.065 --> 00:34:17.440
JONSI STEFANSSON:
So yeah, that's

00:34:17.440 --> 00:34:18.639
actually a great question.

00:34:18.639 --> 00:34:23.699
So we have a scheduler that
is running that is actually

00:34:23.699 --> 00:34:26.190
monitoring if the
controller is congested,

00:34:26.190 --> 00:34:29.190
if it can actually handle
changing the service level.

00:34:29.190 --> 00:34:33.780
If it's congested or can't
handle those 128 megabytes

00:34:33.780 --> 00:34:36.764
a second, it's moved,
and we take care

00:34:36.764 --> 00:34:37.889
of that with the scheduler.

00:34:37.889 --> 00:34:40.510
So everything is automatic.

00:34:40.510 --> 00:34:43.445
It happens automatically.

00:34:43.445 --> 00:34:45.915
AUDIENCE: It's per second,
so the data rate, you're

00:34:45.915 --> 00:34:47.400
going by seconds as well?

00:34:47.400 --> 00:34:49.989
ALIM KARIM: Yes, so our
billing granularity rate now

00:34:49.989 --> 00:34:51.540
is per hour.

00:34:51.540 --> 00:34:54.670
So we monitor capacity
within a given hour.

00:34:54.670 --> 00:34:56.739
We have multiple data
points within the hour

00:34:56.739 --> 00:34:59.380
that we average
out, but the idea

00:34:59.380 --> 00:35:00.856
is to make it even
more granular.

00:35:00.856 --> 00:35:02.230
But we figured,
at least to start

00:35:02.230 --> 00:35:04.870
with, with the pay-go
option, we'll do hourly.

00:35:04.870 --> 00:35:08.180
The monitoring, it will be--
or billing metrics every hour.

00:35:13.040 --> 00:35:15.470
AUDIENCE: So you keep
saying pay-go options.

00:35:15.470 --> 00:35:16.940
What other options are--

00:35:16.940 --> 00:35:18.737
ALIM KARIM: Right, yeah.

00:35:18.737 --> 00:35:19.320
Good question.

00:35:19.320 --> 00:35:21.560
So pay-go basically
is where we want it

00:35:21.560 --> 00:35:22.964
to start, like a cloud service.

00:35:22.964 --> 00:35:24.380
But what we want
to introduce also

00:35:24.380 --> 00:35:27.950
is this notion of being
able to do subscriptions.

00:35:27.950 --> 00:35:30.200
And you can use a different
term, but essentially

00:35:30.200 --> 00:35:33.590
customers that want to commit
for a volume of capacity

00:35:33.590 --> 00:35:37.370
for a given duration, you
get a certain discount.

00:35:37.370 --> 00:35:38.870
What those things
are, we're still

00:35:38.870 --> 00:35:40.578
working through, and
trying to figure out

00:35:40.578 --> 00:35:43.280
what the sweet spots
are, in terms of, is it

00:35:43.280 --> 00:35:46.800
100 terabytes for 12 months, or
is it a petabyte for 12 months,

00:35:46.800 --> 00:35:48.440
and we're going to
introduce those.

00:35:48.440 --> 00:35:51.900
There's always going to
be sort of edge cases,

00:35:51.900 --> 00:35:54.350
but we do want to have
some standard subscription

00:35:54.350 --> 00:35:56.480
options directly
in the cloud that

00:35:56.480 --> 00:35:58.760
said that you have to
use this much capacity,

00:35:58.760 --> 00:36:01.530
and you're committing to use
it for this amount of time.

00:36:01.530 --> 00:36:03.830
You will get billed
monthly, but in exchange

00:36:03.830 --> 00:36:07.134
for that term in volume, you're
going to get a discount of X%.

00:36:07.134 --> 00:36:09.050
JONSI STEFANSSON: We
will just, like, bundle--

00:36:09.050 --> 00:36:11.990
like petabyte bundle.

00:36:11.990 --> 00:36:14.094
That's actually been one
of the feature requests

00:36:14.094 --> 00:36:16.010
from our private preview
customers is actually

00:36:16.010 --> 00:36:19.250
to introduce that as well.

